 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The projection step (Step 0) for 1,000,000 spheres takes 0.22 ms for computation and storage, and 3.2 ms for sorting [S1]. The renderer executes orders of magnitude faster than existing techniques [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The data structures are optimized for GPU cache lines. Intersection Information uses 8 bytes per sphere, and Draw Information uses 32 bytes per sphere [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On an NVIDIA RTX 2080 Ti, Pulsar can optimize a coarse head model (400k spheres) in 73 seconds, compared to 20 minutes for DSS [S1]. In illumination experiments, Pulsar converged in 31 seconds (300 steps) versus 20 minutes for DSS (477 steps) [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering and optimization of millions of spheres; no need for complex acceleration structures; commutative blending allows flexible ordering; tight integration with PyTorch [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Position gradients are unstable for small projected radii (<3 pixels); requires careful handling of numerical inaccuracies in boundary calculations [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When real-time differentiable rendering of large-scale sphere-based representations is required, and when view-dependent appearance is handled via neural shading or static features [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When high-fidelity view-dependent effects are needed without neural shading, or when the scene contains very small primitives that would suffer from gradient instability [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering time for up to one million spheres at 1024x1024 resolution is less than 22 ms; gradient calculation time is less than 6 ms [S1]. For 4.4M spheres at 4K resolution, runtime is 387 ms (forward/backward) on an NVIDIA RTX 2070 GPU [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Moderate memory requirements; 3500 MB for 4.4M spheres at 4K resolution [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** Performance scales favorably with future GPU generations due to the block size limit (256 threads) being determined by current hardware memory constraints [S1]. Rendering speed is largely independent of sphere sizes due to pixel-parallelization [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Orders of magnitude faster than competing techniques (e.g., PyTorch3D sphere renderer reaches 400 ms below 500K spheres, while Pulsar remains below 400 ms at 4.4M spheres) [S1]. Avoids topological inconsistencies and changing topology problems of meshes [S1]. Eliminates the need for acceleration structures (k-d tree, octree) [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires CUDA C and PyTorch integration [S1]. Numerical instability for small projected sphere radii (< 3 pixels) [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When real-time rendering and optimization of millions of spheres are required, or when topological consistency is critical [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** When hardware does not support CUDA or PyTorch, or when very small-scale features (sub-pixel spheres) are critical and cannot be robustified [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Pulsar was introduced in the paper "Pulsar: Efficient Sphere-based Neural Rendering" [S1]. It builds on recent insights in differentiable rendering and neural networks, aiming to limit complexity in the projection step to achieve high speed and scalability [S1]. The method was implemented as open-source software with tight integration with PyTorch, using highly optimized CUDA kernels [S1]. It has been used for applications ranging from 3D reconstruction to general neural rendering, including detailed head models with hair and eyes [S1].
+Pulsar was introduced in 2021 by the authors of "Pulsar: Efficient Sphere-based Neural Rendering" [S1]. It builds on recent insights in differentiable rendering and neural networks, deliberately limiting complexity in the projection step to obtain high speed and scalability [S1]. It is implemented as a C++ and CUDA C PyTorch extension [S1]. The method was developed to alleviate challenges in gradient-based optimization of neural mesh, voxel, or function representations, such as topological inconsistencies, high memory footprints, and slow rendering speeds [S1].
 
